{"causes":["The establishment of the FICAT in the 1990s aimed to address the need for a standardized international terminology in anatomy.","The development of new medical technologies and treatments in the 20th century created a need for clear and consistent anatomical descriptions.","The increasing globalization of medical education and research in the late 20th century required the development of a standardized language for anatomical description."],"effects":["The adoption of standardized terminology in medical education and research has improved the accuracy and clarity of anatomical descriptions.","The widespread use of the Terminologia Anatomica has facilitated international collaboration and the advancement of scientific knowledge in anatomy and medicine.","The text's influence has led to the development of new technologies and treatments, such as 3D printing and robotic surgery, which rely on accurate and consistent anatomical descriptions."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"Federative International Committee on Anatomical Terminology","sourceName":"Federative International Committee on Anatomical Terminology","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"terminologia-anatomica","targetName":"Terminologia Anatomica","context":"The FICAT created the Terminologia Anatomica as a standardized international terminology in anatomy."},{"sourceSlug":"World Health Organization","sourceName":"World Health Organization","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"terminologia-anatomica","targetName":"Terminologia Anatomica","context":"The WHO adopted the Terminologia Anatomica as a standard for anatomical terminology in medical education and research."},{"sourceSlug":"International Organization for Standardization","sourceName":"International Organization for Standardization","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"terminologia-anatomica","targetName":"Terminologia Anatomica","context":"The ISO recognized the Terminologia Anatomica as a global standard for anatomical terminology."},{"sourceSlug":"American Medical Association","sourceName":"American Medical Association","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"terminologia-anatomica","targetName":"Terminologia Anatomica","context":"The AMA recommends the use of the Terminologia Anatomica in medical education and research."},{"sourceSlug":"National Institutes of Health","sourceName":"National Institutes of Health","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"terminologia-anatomica","targetName":"Terminologia Anatomica","context":"The NIH funded research projects that developed and validated the Terminologia Anatomica."},{"sourceSlug":"European Union","sourceName":"European Union","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"terminologia-anatomica","targetName":"Terminologia Anatomica","context":"The EU supported the development and implementation of the Terminologia Anatomica in European medical education and research."},{"sourceSlug":"National Academy of Sciences","sourceName":"National Academy of Sciences","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"terminologia-anatomica","targetName":"Terminologia Anatomica","context":"The NAS recommends the use of the Terminologia Anatomica in scientific research and education."},{"sourceSlug":"International Society for Phylogenetics and Molecular Biology","sourceName":"International Society for Phylogenetics and Molecular Biology","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"terminologia-anatomica","targetName":"Terminologia Anatomica","context":"The ISMB recognizes the Terminologia Anatomica as a standard for anatomical terminology in molecular biology and phylogenetics."},{"sourceSlug":"American Association of Anatomists","sourceName":"American Association of Anatomists","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"terminologia-anatomica","targetName":"Terminologia Anatomica","context":"The AANA endorses the use of the Terminologia Anatomica in anatomical education and research."}],"places":[{"name":"Geneva, Switzerland","role":"Global Hub"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Terminologia Anatomica","type":"Reference article","slug":"terminologia-anatomica"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q286567","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Terminologia_Anatomica"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Terminologia Anatomica contributed to the historical record of the Contemporary period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"Terminologia Anatomica (TA) is the international standard of anatomical nomenclature — the authoritative list of standard anatomical terms for human gross anatomy — published by the Federative International Programme on Anatomical Terminologies (FIPAT, formerly FICAT) on behalf of the International Federation of Associations of Anatomists (IFAA). The current standard is Terminologia Anatomica 2 (TA2), published in 2019, superseding the first edition (TA1, 1998), which itself replaced the previous standard Nomina Anatomica (originally published 1895, last revised 1989). Terminologia Anatomica 2 contains approximately 7,500 terms designating structures of human gross anatomy in Latin (with English equivalents), organised hierarchically by body region and system.

Anatomical nomenclature — the systematic naming of anatomical structures — is one of the oldest branches of scientific terminology, with its origins in ancient Greek medicine (Galen, Herophilus) and its first systematic codification in the Renaissance anatomical tradition (Vesalius's De Humani Corporis Fabrica, 1543). The Basel Nomina Anatomica (BNA, 1895) was the first internationally agreed standard, produced by a commission of 50 anatomists at the Anatomische Gesellschaft meeting in Basel — replacing a chaos of competing national and personal naming traditions with a single Latin standard. The subsequent international revision processes (the Jena Nomina Anatomica 1935, the Paris Nomina Anatomica 1955, the revisions of 1961, 1966, 1977, 1983, and 1989, and the Terminologia Anatomica 1998 and 2019) represent a century-long process of international scientific collaboration to standardise the language of anatomy.

Terminologia Anatomica matters both as a practical scientific standard (enabling communication across languages in anatomy, surgery, radiology, and clinical medicine) and as a symbol of the internationalisation of science — the aspiration to replace the diversity of national terminological traditions with a single universal language of anatomy, accessible to medical practitioners worldwide.","subjects":["Medical Science","Modern Era","Anatomy","Scientific Nomenclature","Medical Education","Latin","International Standards","Clinical Medicine"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The Terminologia Anatomica has had a profound impact on the fields of medicine, biology, and science, facilitating international collaboration and advancing our understanding of the human body.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
